From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] CD: the EE and the IOP number the disc's sectors the same way

What broke:
  Ben 10: Protector of Earth ran at 3-4 frames per second, even in its
  menus, on Linux and on a Windows Release build. Sampling the game thread
  showed it spent much of its time in the IOP's sceCdRead: the game's
  CDARB module (run in the IOP interpreter) read the same 8 sectors at LSN
  2046146 about 2,000 times a second. The IOP's virtual disc has only
  1,200,598 sectors, so every read failed and CDARB retried forever.

Why:
  The EE and the IOP each make up their own sector numbers for the files
  of the extracted disc. The EE's sceCdSearchFile (Kernel/Stubs) hands out
  "pseudo" LSNs from 0x100000 in search order; the IOP's cdvdman builds a
  virtual ISO 9660 image of the same folder, where GAME.WAD starts at LSN
  40. The game asks for GAME.WAD's LSN on the EE and then passes absolute
  LSNs (the WAD start plus a file's offset) to CDARB, which serves the
  audio streams (the menu music, 99_91.pss) from the IOP. The EE's LSN
  pointed past the end of the IOP's disc.
  A second, smaller cost: each failed read also checked 16 KB of IOP RAM
  ownership one byte at a time (IopMemory::ownsRamRange, std::all_of).

The fix:
  - IopSubsystem::findCdFile(path) (through IopEmulator and IopCdvd)
    returns where the IOP's virtual disc puts a file: LSN, size and the
    volume size. It answers only for the virtual disc, not when the IOP
    reads a real disc image.
  - PS2Runtime::findIopCdFile forwards to it, and sceCdSearchFile passes
    the result to registerCdFile, which then uses the IOP's LSN instead of
    a pseudo one. Files the IOP does not know keep pseudo LSNs, now past
    the end of the IOP's volume so the two kinds cannot overlap.
    With no runtime (unit tests) the old pseudo numbering is unchanged.
  - IopMemory::ownsRamRange uses memchr for the zero byte.
  Tested in the game: GAME.WAD is now found at LSN 40 on both sides, the
  repeated reads stopped, and the menus went from 3-4 to 5-6 frames per
  second (findings/19). New ps2xTest case "the IOP's virtual disc reports
  where a cdrom0 file is"; the test host gained a cdRoot field.

